<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barings Bank</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1762">1762</a>–<a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Baring Brothers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Barings Bank, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1762, was one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s oldest merchant banks and a leading lender to governments in the nineteenth century, helping to finance the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a>. Its heavy lending in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> brought it close to collapse in the crisis of 1890. It failed in 1995 after huge losses from unauthorised trading by Nick Leeson in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Barings Bank began as the John and Francis Baring Company, founded by Sir Francis Baring with his older brother John as a mostly silent partner. They were sons of a wool trader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter-new-hampshire">Exeter</a> born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>. The firm started in offices off Cheapside and moved to Mincing Lane, diversifying from wool into other commodities, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, which enriched the family considerably. Its success owed much to a network of corresponding houses, above all Hope and Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. In 1774 it began business in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1802 Barings and Hope and Company facilitated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a>. After a down payment of 3 million dollars in gold, the remainder was paid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> bonds, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> sold to Barings through Hope at 87.50 per 100 of face value, with Alexander Baring making the arrangements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Barings also helped finance the United States government in the War of 1812, and by 1818 it was called the sixth great European power. In the 1820s and 1830s it grew entangled in the slave-powered economy through mortgages on enslaved people and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> cotton plantations. It was appointed in 1845 to supply <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> to Ireland for famine relief.</p><p>Overexposure to Argentine and Uruguayan debt led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panic-of-1890">Panic of 1890</a>. William Lidderdale, governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-england">Bank of England</a>, arranged a consortium to bail the bank out, and a limited liability company took over its viable business. Barings never regained its dominant position.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The bank was brought down by Nick Leeson, its derivatives trader in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>. He hid his losses in an error account numbered 88888, and his positions were hit by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobe">Kobe</a> earthquake. The losses reached 827 million pounds, twice the bank's available trading capital. Barings was declared insolvent on 26 February 1995. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> bank ING bought it for one pound and took over its liabilities. Leeson was captured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> on 2 March and sentenced in Singapore to six years and six months in prison. The Barings name survives in an asset management business owned by MassMutual.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Barings Bank” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
